WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.100 --> 00:00:02.691
Jenna: My road trip is coming to an end

00:00:02.691 --> 00:00:04.440
in Hot Springs, Arkansas,

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.900
the only place in the
world where beer is brewed

00:00:06.900 --> 00:00:08.830
using thermal spring water.

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:10.540
Keep watching to learn how it's made

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:13.447
on the season finale of
Travel Dares: Season US.

00:00:15.631 --> 00:00:17.850
(upbeat music)

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:20.000
Last episode, I got my final clue

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:21.940
for where I'd end up on this episode.

00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.930
Check out these hoppy hot springs.

00:00:24.930 --> 00:00:27.680
So now I'm here in Hot Springs, Arkansas.

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:30.590
This city may be known for
its world famous bathhouses

00:00:30.590 --> 00:00:32.267
and National Park, but today,

00:00:32.267 --> 00:00:34.530
instead of bathing in
thermal spring water,

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:35.823
I'm going to be drinking it.

00:00:37.017 --> 00:00:38.890
I've been to Arkansas before,

00:00:38.890 --> 00:00:40.650
but I've never been to Hot Springs.

00:00:40.650 --> 00:00:43.647
I am so excited to be making beer today.

00:00:43.647 --> 00:00:45.078
Hey, I'm Jenna.

00:00:45.078 --> 00:00:46.988
Rose: Hey Jenna, I'm Rose,
and welcome to Superior.

00:00:46.988 --> 00:00:48.260
Jenna: What a beautiful space.

00:00:48.260 --> 00:00:49.160
Rose: Thank you very much.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:50.950
Jenna: So I'd love to know
a little bit of history

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:51.850
about this place.

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:53.969
Rose: Superior Bathhouse
was built in 1916.

00:00:53.969 --> 00:00:55.420
And it's pretty interesting:

00:00:55.420 --> 00:00:57.970
It was on the site of a
previously built bathhouse

00:00:57.970 --> 00:00:59.730
that had been built in the late 1800s.

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:00.960
In the '60s and '70s,

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:03.079
most of these bathhouses
closed to the public.

00:01:03.079 --> 00:01:05.720
So the Superior closed in 1983,

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:07.180
and then in the early 2000s,

00:01:07.180 --> 00:01:09.590
the National Park
Service started a program

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:12.131
to attract small businesses
to take over the buildings.

00:01:12.131 --> 00:01:14.299
So I'm one of the adaptive reuse projects

00:01:14.299 --> 00:01:15.683
here in the National Park.

00:01:15.683 --> 00:01:18.402
Jenna: What inspired you
to want to make beer?

00:01:18.402 --> 00:01:19.551
Rose: Well, I've always loved beer,

00:01:19.551 --> 00:01:21.037
and I used to be a home brewer.

00:01:21.037 --> 00:01:23.623
When I moved to Hot Springs in 2011,

00:01:23.623 --> 00:01:26.127
I was surprised that there was
not a brewery here already.

00:01:26.127 --> 00:01:28.250
Hot Springs National Park is famous for

00:01:28.250 --> 00:01:30.400
our thermal spring water
because you can drink it.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:32.430
So I figured if you can
drink the famous water,

00:01:32.430 --> 00:01:33.671
you might as well make beer out of it.

00:01:33.671 --> 00:01:35.380
Jenna: That's great, I love that.

00:01:35.380 --> 00:01:36.310
Well, I'm really excited,

00:01:36.310 --> 00:01:38.120
I would love to see
how you guys make beer.

00:01:38.120 --> 00:01:39.010
Rose: Well, today's brew day,

00:01:39.010 --> 00:01:41.162
so let's head on down to the
warehouse and get started.

00:01:41.162 --> 00:01:41.995
Jenna: Ah, yes.

00:01:41.995 --> 00:01:44.578
(upbeat music)

00:02:00.157 --> 00:02:03.407
(truck engine roaring)

00:02:09.093 --> 00:02:11.926
Rose: Let's line that up.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:15.910
Rose: All right, let's
hop up on the brew deck,

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:17.160
and I'll walk you through the process.

00:02:17.160 --> 00:02:18.570
Jenna: Sounds great.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:20.919
Rose: Now the first step in
making beer is to heat up,

00:02:20.919 --> 00:02:22.240
like I said, a lot of water.

00:02:22.240 --> 00:02:24.620
Once we mix the grains with the hot water,

00:02:24.620 --> 00:02:27.644
the goal is to extract
the flavor and the color

00:02:27.644 --> 00:02:29.850
and the sugar from those grains.

00:02:29.850 --> 00:02:32.110
That's why we had to crush them earlier.

00:02:32.110 --> 00:02:33.950
So now they're at the perfect consistency,

00:02:33.950 --> 00:02:36.564
perfect size to get that
flavor out of the grains.

00:02:36.564 --> 00:02:39.799
And when that's done, it gets
pumped over to this vessel.

00:02:39.799 --> 00:02:41.268
This is called the boil kettle.

00:02:41.268 --> 00:02:42.750
So as you can probably imagine,

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.930
this is where we bring
the future beer to a boil.

00:02:45.930 --> 00:02:47.666
Once it comes to a boil, we add hops.

00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:49.539
Hops are like the seasoning in beer,

00:02:49.539 --> 00:02:52.390
but they provide bitterness,
and traditionally,

00:02:52.390 --> 00:02:54.910
they provide kind of a
preservative effect to beer.

00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:56.720
So that's why they've
always been used in beer.

00:02:56.720 --> 00:02:58.536
But today, we use hops as a flavoring.

00:02:58.536 --> 00:02:59.927
Jenna: I did not know that.

00:02:59.927 --> 00:03:01.800
Rose: So after we're done over here today,

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:04.450
we pump the liquid, it's
called wort at that point.

00:03:04.450 --> 00:03:06.780
Once the wort is in the fermenter,

00:03:06.780 --> 00:03:07.820
that's when we add the yeast,

00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:09.780
yeast provides flavor to the beer,

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:12.603
it produces CO2, which is
the bubbles, the carbonation,

00:03:12.603 --> 00:03:15.568
and most importantly,
yeast makes the alcohol.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:20.270
What we're making today will
be ready in about two weeks.

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.258
But if you want, we can go
try one from the last batch.

00:03:22.258 --> 00:03:23.390
Jenna: Yes.

00:03:23.390 --> 00:03:24.223
Rose: Yeah.

00:03:24.223 --> 00:03:25.056
Jenna: I would love that.

00:03:25.056 --> 00:03:25.889
Rose: All right, let's go.

00:03:25.889 --> 00:03:28.760
(lively music)

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:30.610
All right, Jenna, well,
I'm enjoying my elixir,

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:31.443
but if you don't mind,

00:03:31.443 --> 00:03:32.550
I went ahead and ordered you a flight

00:03:32.550 --> 00:03:34.380
so you can sample a couple of our beers.

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.213
Jenna: Thank you so much.

00:03:35.213 --> 00:03:36.101
Rose: Oh, you're welcome.

00:03:36.101 --> 00:03:36.934
(both laughing)

00:03:36.934 --> 00:03:39.040
Jenna: That is quite the flight.

00:03:39.040 --> 00:03:40.130
Thank you so much.

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:40.963
Waitress: You're welcome.

00:03:40.963 --> 00:03:41.990
Rose: Well, we call this a beer bath.

00:03:41.990 --> 00:03:44.453
It is a flight of all 18
beers that we have on tap.

00:03:44.453 --> 00:03:46.117
Jenna: 18 different beers.

00:03:46.117 --> 00:03:48.570
Rose: So I would love for
you to try our oatmeal stout,

00:03:48.570 --> 00:03:50.290
that's called the Foul Play Stout.

00:03:50.290 --> 00:03:51.123
Jenna: Oatmeal.

00:03:51.123 --> 00:03:52.170
Rose: Oatmeal stout.

00:03:52.170 --> 00:03:53.003
Jenna: OK.

00:03:53.003 --> 00:03:53.836
Rose: Here you go.

00:03:55.044 --> 00:03:55.940
What do you think?

00:03:55.940 --> 00:03:57.515
Rose: Nice and chocolatey.
Jenna: Oh, I love that, yes.

00:03:57.515 --> 00:04:00.030
Rose: OK, if we want to create
some contrast from that,

00:04:00.030 --> 00:04:02.941
you need to try our Dill Pickle Sour.

00:04:02.941 --> 00:04:03.795
Jenna: Pickle?

00:04:03.795 --> 00:04:06.490
Rose: Mm-hmm, yep, it's
going to be tart and tangy,

00:04:06.490 --> 00:04:09.010
but a definite distinct
flavor of dill pickle.

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:10.992
Jenna: Dill pickle.

00:04:10.992 --> 00:04:12.613
Rose: Dill Pickle Sour.

00:04:16.740 --> 00:04:18.260
Jenna: That is pickley.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:19.160
Rose: Taste that pickle?

00:04:19.160 --> 00:04:19.993
Jenna: I really like that.

00:04:19.993 --> 00:04:22.340
Rose: I know it's sour and
it's kind of salty, too.

00:04:22.340 --> 00:04:24.120
Jenna: Yeah, that is really interesting.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:25.320
Rose: Do you want another crazy one?

00:04:25.320 --> 00:04:26.190
Jenna: Yeah, oh yeah.

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:27.700
Rose: OK, try our Spicy Ride.

00:04:27.700 --> 00:04:29.348
That's number four, right here.

00:04:29.348 --> 00:04:32.640
It's the ride and it's spicy, I guess.

00:04:32.640 --> 00:04:35.690
Jenna: That is, it
lives up to the promise.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:38.272
Rose: It does, we use a whole
case of jalapeños per batch.

00:04:38.272 --> 00:04:39.630
The cool thing about beer is

00:04:39.630 --> 00:04:43.370
you can create any flavor your
mind's eye can come up with.

00:04:43.370 --> 00:04:44.260
It's like being an artist,

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.848
we have a palette of
ingredients to work with,

00:04:45.848 --> 00:04:48.180
different grains, different yeast, hops,

00:04:48.180 --> 00:04:51.100
and different, even
vegetables, in this case.

00:04:51.100 --> 00:04:53.110
Jenna: So what do you want
people's takeaway to be

00:04:53.110 --> 00:04:54.355
when they come here and visit?

00:04:54.355 --> 00:04:56.148
Rose: Well, I'm so happy
you chose Hot Springs

00:04:56.148 --> 00:04:59.070
as part of your trip because
I take every opportunity I can

00:04:59.070 --> 00:05:00.840
to tell the world about what a fun

00:05:00.840 --> 00:05:03.167
and funky little town we have here.

00:05:03.167 --> 00:05:04.920
And this town is all about the water.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:07.110
So the water is the reason
we have a National Park,

00:05:07.110 --> 00:05:08.730
and it's the reason this bathhouse exists,

00:05:08.730 --> 00:05:10.056
it's the reason we have a brewery here.

00:05:10.056 --> 00:05:12.180
So that's the reason
you're drinking our beer.

00:05:12.180 --> 00:05:13.280
It's all about the water.

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:14.940
And the water has brought people together

00:05:14.940 --> 00:05:17.040
for centuries here to this area.

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.370
It's so cool that today, this is a place

00:05:19.370 --> 00:05:21.244
where people can come and
just have a great time

00:05:21.244 --> 00:05:22.770
in our small town.

00:05:22.770 --> 00:05:24.240
Jenna: That's so cool.

00:05:24.240 --> 00:05:25.272
Jenna: Cheers to that.
Rose: Cheers.

00:05:25.272 --> 00:05:27.855
(bright music)

00:05:30.172 --> 00:05:33.420
Jenna: I had such an amazing
time meeting Rose today

00:05:33.420 --> 00:05:35.235
and the amount of passion she brings

00:05:35.235 --> 00:05:39.260
into the craft of making
beer is so inspiring.

00:05:39.260 --> 00:05:40.958
This is definitely one
of those experiences

00:05:40.958 --> 00:05:42.635
you can't miss out on.

00:05:42.635 --> 00:05:45.710
This road trip has been incredible.

00:05:45.710 --> 00:05:48.043
It's hard to say what my favorites are.

00:05:48.043 --> 00:05:51.548
The most eye-opening part about this trip

00:05:51.548 --> 00:05:54.215
was probably Qualla [Arts &amp;
Crafts Mutual] and learning

00:05:54.215 --> 00:05:56.908
about a culture that's
so different than my own.

00:05:56.908 --> 00:05:59.518
I think the most exciting adventure

00:05:59.518 --> 00:06:01.670
I've had was guitar making.

00:06:01.670 --> 00:06:02.647
It was awesome.

00:06:02.647 --> 00:06:04.579
And as someone who plays
an acoustic guitar,

00:06:04.579 --> 00:06:07.706
getting an opportunity to play
an electric with a legend,

00:06:07.706 --> 00:06:09.858
uh, I loved every second of that.

00:06:09.858 --> 00:06:12.960
The most thrilling part
of this road trip was

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:14.454
definitely Ruby Falls,

00:06:14.454 --> 00:06:18.160
trekking through a cave so far underground

00:06:18.160 --> 00:06:22.190
to come upon this breathtaking,
majestic waterfall

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:25.610
was an experience I will never forget.

00:06:25.610 --> 00:06:28.810
And I think the most surprising
part of this road trip

00:06:28.810 --> 00:06:30.367
was President's Park.

00:06:30.367 --> 00:06:34.358
Coming upon those giant
president heads was like,

00:06:34.358 --> 00:06:38.430
whoa, I had no idea that even existed.

00:06:38.430 --> 00:06:41.120
I hope this season of Travel
Dares has inspired you

00:06:41.120 --> 00:06:42.920
to go on your own road trip.

00:06:42.920 --> 00:06:45.385
There are so many incredible
places in this country

00:06:45.385 --> 00:06:47.299
worth seeing for yourself.

00:06:47.299 --> 00:06:48.482
(bright music continues)

